//! `rtm` library target — real-time transaction monitoring, Nirdosha v2
//! dialect demo. Holds the actual policy/catalog corpus (one module per
//! file from `examples/rtm/roles-N-guard_policy.md`'s own breakdown) so
//! both `src/main.nir` (the `[[bin]]`) and `tests/guarded_ingest.rs`
//! (integration tests, which Cargo always links against a package's own
//! `[lib]` target) see the *same* registered `nirdosha_guard_registry`
//! records at runtime.
//!
//! This split exists only because `linkme::distributed_slice` registration
//! is a link-time effect: it only happens inside whichever binary/test
//! process actually links the code declaring it (see
//! `nirdosha_guard_registry::write_dump_from_env`'s own doc comment). A
//! `[[bin]]`-only package has no `[lib]` for its `tests/*.rs` to link
//! against, so putting the corpus directly under `main.nir` (this crate's
//! very first shape) left every integration test seeing zero registered
//! policies — not a bug in the corpus, a missing library target.
//!
// `#[classify]`'s and `lineage_query!`'s generated static/type names don't
// screaming-case the source item's own identifier (a pre-existing, purely
// cosmetic naming-convention gap in those macros — see
// `crates/nirdosha-rt/tests/rtm_policy_corpus.rs` for the identical
// suppression). Dead-code warnings are expected: this pass registers the
// policy/catalog corpus for real but doesn't wire a runtime consumer for
// every declared dataset/relation/reference/entity type — `tests/
// guarded_ingest.rs` proves the guarded read/write path for the flows this
// phase actually wires end to end.
#![allow(non_upper_case_globals, non_camel_case_types, dead_code)]

